WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.728
Reading a big diff efficiently.

2
00:00:02.008 --> 00:00:08.984
You can’t read every line of every agent diff with equal care, so read in an order that puts your attention where risk is.

3
00:00:09.264 --> 00:00:10.352
Triage first.

4
00:00:10.632 --> 00:00:15.475
git diff --stat (or the file list in your review tool) shows what changed and how much.

5
00:00:15.755 --> 00:00:18.229
Compare it with the spec before reading any code.

6
00:00:18.509 --> 00:00:20.877
Unexpected files are the first finding.

7
00:00:21.157 --> 00:00:22.395
Tests before code.

8
00:00:22.675 --> 00:00:27.347
Read the test changes and confirm they encode the requirement (lesson 7).

9
00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:32.213
Then you can read the code asking “does this satisfy these tests for the right reason?

10
00:00:32.493 --> 00:00:34.307
” rather than “what is this doing?

11
00:00:34.587 --> 00:00:34.884
”.

12
00:00:35.164 --> 00:00:36.551
Risky files next.

13
00:00:36.831 --> 00:00:46.559
Anything on checklist items 3 to 5: auth, payments, data deletion, migrations, dependency manifests, CI and agent configuration.

14
00:00:46.839 --> 00:00:48.375
Read these line by line.

15
00:00:48.655 --> 00:00:51.108
Mechanical changes last, and separately.

16
00:00:51.388 --> 00:00:54.183
Renames and formatting hide logic changes.

17
00:00:54.463 --> 00:01:01.332
Ask for them in separate commits, and use git diff -w to ignore whitespace-only changes when reading logic.

18
00:01:01.612 --> 00:01:02.338
Run it.

19
00:01:02.618 --> 00:01:07.866
Check out the branch and exercise the behavior, especially for user-facing changes.

20
00:01:08.146 --> 00:01:19.026
Claude Code’s bundled /verify skill builds and runs the app to look at the result, which catches problems tests miss, but you should still look yourself at anything a user will see.

21
00:01:19.306 --> 00:01:21.460
Ask for evidence, not reassurance.

22
00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.242
If the agent’s summary says “handled the edge case,” find the test that proves it.
